## Done

#903 — commented and closed. The one issue my commits actually resolved.

It asked a design question (vanish or rank last?), and Task 1 decided rank last and implemented it: the entry gate moved from score > 0.0 to positive_signal > 0.0, which sums only W_NEVER_GRADED and stale_weight(..) — established by reading the constants, not by trusting the W_* names. Ordering still uses the full score, so within-tier order is byte-identical, and no weight constant moved (changing one would have relocated the knife-edge rather than removed it).

Three things I checked rather than asserted before posting:
• The gate really is positive_signal > 0.0 at src/commands_risk_epistemic.rs:669.
• The old test was inverted, not deleted (..._drops_a_file_... → ..._ranks_last_rather_than_vanishing), keeping both anti-vacuous arithmetic rows.
• The live reading — and it is not a zero diff, which the task had pre-registered as an acceptable outcome. src/safety.rs and src/commands_info.rs now rank at -0.1. So the change admits a wider class than the exact-zero knife-edge: anything fully cancelled or over-cancelled. That's what the implemented rule says; reporting it as a surprise would be dishonest, and burying it would be worse.

A Day-179 prediction discharged itself in passing: that entry said PartiallyGraded was "unobservable on the file that motivated it — it will show up the moment src/safety.rs goes stale again." It has, and it does.

